# Shell scripts MUST use LF endings — they run inside Linux containers (e.g. the feature-026
# mc-service/mongo-entrypoint.sh, opensearch/init-audit-user.sh). A CRLF shebang/line makes the
# container fail with "bad interpreter" / "\r" errors. Force LF regardless of a contributor's
# core.autocrlf on Windows.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The same reasoning, one layer up: the rule above exists because a CRLF line breaks an
# *interpreter*; these exist because a CRLF line breaks a *parser*. The CI gate scripts read these
# file types as text with line-oriented parsers (no js-yaml — the guardrails suite runs before any
# install step), and two gates were silently mis-verdicting on a CRLF working tree:
#
#   - check-ci-digest-coverage.mjs failed CLOSED — split('\n') left a trailing \r, the
#     `# ci-digest-exempt:` marker pattern could not match, and three correctly-exempt jobs were
#     reported as uncovered. That is the failure PRD §1.3 reported as "unexplained".
#   - check-openwiki-okf.mjs failed OPEN — Date.parse() on an untrimmed timestamp returned NaN, so
#     the staleness check silently never ran and the gate printed "✅ conformant".
#
# Feature 051 also made both parsers carriage-return tolerant, so a gate's verdict never depends on
# a contributor's core.autocrlf. This declaration stops the condition being produced in the first
# place; the tolerant parsing stops the next parser inheriting the trap. Both layers, deliberately.
#
# NOTE for existing Windows clones: this governs future checkouts, not files already in a working
# tree. Re-normalize once with `git rm --cached -r . && git reset --hard`.
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf

# Added after a WINDOWS measurement on 2026-08-10 found the same defect class one file type
# further out. The rules above were scoped to "what the CI gates parse", but the real class is
# ANY file whose CONTENT is read and split by code — and a guard test reads TypeScript SOURCE:
# e2e-collection-ownership.guard.test.mjs does `fn.indexOf('
}
')` over e2e-cleanup.ts. On a CRLF
# checkout that returns -1, the extracted body collapsed to two characters, and the guard failed for
# a reason that had nothing to do with what it guards. Scoping this list by "which tool parses it"
# was the mistake; scope it by "is it parsed at all".
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.cjs text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# ── Test fixtures keep their EXACT bytes (feature 052) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# `*.md text eol=lf` above normalizes on add. That is right for real documents and WRONG for a
# fixture whose CRLF *is the subject under test*:
# scripts/__tests__/fixtures/openwiki-governance/whitespace-drift/ exists to prove
# check-openwiki-governance normalizes line endings, and its sibling test asserts a passing exit.
# Strip the CRLF and that test still PASSES while no longer testing CRLF at all — a silently
# weakened gate, not a visible break. Found when a `git filter-branch` re-added the fixture and
# normalized it (052); the rule that follows makes it unrepeatable.
scripts/__tests__/fixtures/** -text
